{
  "program": "CBSTM03B",
  "caseId": "case12",
  "description": "The CUSTFILE keyed read that HITS, and the one case in this folder that pins the whole 500-byte customer record field by field. app/cbl/CBSTM03B.CBL:118 EVALUATE LK-M03B-DD reaches WHEN 'CUSTFILE' at :123 - the third dispatch arm, so 'TRNXFILE' (:119) and 'XREFFILE' (:121) are passed over first - and :124 performs 3000-CUSTFILE-PROC THRU 3999-EXIT. Call one is the OPEN the session needs: IF M03B-OPEN (:183) true, OPEN INPUT CUST-FILE (:184), GO TO 3900-EXIT (:185), and :201 MOVE CUSTFILE-STATUS TO LK-M03B-RC reports '00'. Call two is the subject: IF M03B-OPEN (:183) is now FALSE, so control reaches IF M03B-READ-K (:188) TRUE, then :189 MOVE LK-M03B-KEY (1:LK-M03B-KEY-LN) TO FD-CUST-ID, :190 READ CUST-FILE INTO LK-M03B-FLDT, :192 GO TO 3900-EXIT and :201 again - so the status is '00' on CUSTFILE-STATUS specifically, the third of the four independent two-byte areas at :83-97, which is what a per-DD status assertion means. That guard order is behaviour, not arrangement: the CLOSE arm at :195 is never reached because :192 has already left the paragraph. The reference-modified MOVE at :189 is the statement worth reading twice. LK-M03B-KEY (1:LK-M03B-KEY-LN) takes the first NINE bytes of the 25-byte key field, and the receiver FD-CUST-ID is PIC X(09) at :72 - ALPHANUMERIC - so the nine bytes fill it exactly, with no padding and no realignment, and the lookup key is the literal string '000000050'. The identical-looking statement at :214 moves into FD-ACCT-ID PIC 9(11) at :77, a NUMERIC-DISPLAY receiver, where the same syntax performs a numeric move with left zero-fill instead; cases 13, 14, 16 and 17 pin that half. One statement, two move rules, decided entirely by the receiver's PICTURE - and both are reproduced rather than unified. The length itself is not a guess: app/cbl/CBSTM03A.CBL:368-377 drives this call, and :374 COMPUTE WS-M03B-KEY-LN = LENGTH OF XREF-CUST-ID takes it from a PIC 9(09) field in app/cpy/CVACT03Y.cpy, so it is nine. It is pinned as the signed zoned image '000I' because LK-M03B-KEY-LN is PIC S9(4) DISPLAY (:111) and a positive sign is overpunched into the low-order byte rather than occupying a fifth one - the same reason the OPEN's zero is '000{'. LK-M03B-KEY comes back unmodified at 25 bytes, left-justified and space-padded, because MOVE XREF-CUST-ID TO WS-M03B-KEY (:372) moves PIC 9(09) into PIC X(25) under the alphanumeric rule and the subroutine never writes back to the key field. READ ... INTO moves the 500-byte record into LK-M03B-FLDT PIC X(1000): left-justified and space-filled, so bytes 1-500 are the record and 501-1000 are spaces. The key is not invented. '000000050' is the XREF-CUST-ID of row 0 of app/data/ASCII/cardxref.txt, whose XREF-CARD-NUM 0500024453765740 is the card the TRNXFILE seeds of cases 1 to 6 carry and whose row case 8 decodes; it is the value the statement flow actually carries out of the cross-reference and into 2000-CUSTFILE-GET. The record decodes through app/cpy/CUSTREC.cpy (statement.model.Stm03CustomerRecord) and never through app/cpy/CVCUS01Y.cpy - CBSTM03A copies CUSTREC at :55 and moves LK-M03B-FLDT into that CUSTOMER-RECORD at :388 - so the date field is named CUST-DOB-YYYYMMDD, with no hyphens between the date components. CVCUS01Y names the very same span with hyphens there instead, and that one field name is the ONLY difference between two otherwise byte-identical layouts. They are kept apart precisely so field-for-field diffing stays meaningful, so the hyphenated spelling is deliberately absent from this file altogether - carrying it even in prose would put the wrong name one careless copy away from the expectation map. Note also that the VALUE in that field is the hyphenated text 1960-12-01 even though the field NAME carries none - a legacy oddity preserved rather than tidied. All nineteen spans are pinned in declaration order, including the trailing FILLER X(168) as 168 spaces: FILLER is an emitted span and not a gap, and it is what takes the record to 9+25+25+25+50+50+50+2+3+10+15+15+9+20+10+10+1+3+168 = 500. Omit it and every offset after it shifts while the total silently falls to 332. CUST-ID, CUST-SSN and CUST-FICO-CREDIT-SCORE are PIC 9 fields carried as characters so their leading zeros survive. All fifty fixture rows are seeded and all fifty are pinned, which is what makes '000000050' the row at zero-based index 49 - the fiftieth of custdata.txt's ascending 000000001 to 000000050 - and is at the same time the read-only proof: every SELECT is OPEN INPUT (:136, :160, :184, :209) and no paragraph writes, so a row that came back different from the way it went in would be a parity failure no area image could reveal. The caller's verdict stays with the caller. CBSTM03B has no DISPLAY of its own and never assigns RETURN-CODE - it GOBACKs without touching it - which is why the expected return code is 0 and no message is expected; LK-M03B-RC is a FILE STATUS, not a return code. And CBSTM03A's EVALUATE at :379-386 has deliberately NO '10' arm for this DD: it distinguishes '00' from WHEN OTHER only, so any status other than '00' would take DISPLAY 'ERROR READING CUSTFILE' and 9999-ABEND-PROGRAM. This case takes the '00' arm; case 11 supplies the '23' miss that the OTHER arm would act on. 'W' (:107) and 'Z' (:108) remain declared and are SET nowhere in app/cbl, so this case does not drive them and leaving them unexercised is deliberate. Provenance: every expected value here is STATICALLY DERIVED - from the line numbers above, from the CUSTREC offsets, from the LK-M03B-AREA widths 8+1+2+25+4+1000 = 1040 at :100-112, and from the real bytes of app/data/ASCII/custdata.txt row 49 - and was never captured from an execution of the COBOL. Section 0.7.6 of the plan records eight independently verified blockers to running it in this environment and 0.9.11 escalates the substituted provenance as risk R-A; the twenty cases per program, the field-for-field diffing and the diff-count-zero gate are all unchanged. HOW THE CALL SCRIPT IS DECLARED RATHER THAN HELD IN JAVA: the sequence of CALLs below used to live in a Java switch over this file's case identifier, which meant the case file did not describe the one thing it asserts - a subroutine whose entire contract is a DD name plus a one-character operation code IS its call sequence. Renumbering this file would have silently given it a different script, and a twenty-first case would have thrown from a default arm. unitStimulus.operationScript now carries it, one entry per CALL in issue order, each declaring LK-M03B-DD, the 88-level operation code from app/cbl/CBSTM03B.cbl:103-108, and where applicable LK-M03B-KEY and LK-M03B-KEY-LN. Two caller idioms are declared explicitly because the source distinguishes them: status '00' is the MOVE ZERO TO WS-M03B-RC every real call site issues, and its ABSENCE carries the previous call's status forward - the only way to observe a fall-through exit that assigns nothing new; primesRecordArea true is the MOVE SPACES TO WS-M03B-FLDT that app/cbl/CBSTM03A.CBL:L350, :L745 and :L834 issue, and its ABSENCE is the omission at :L857-860 that makes 'at end of file the record area is returned unchanged' an assertion with content. Every expected area image below was derived from this script by reading CBSTM03B's rules, never by running the Java.",
